            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Yes, that's a nice hint. I will play with that for a while. Currently trying to come up with something that looks like _nord_, but suits my eyes even better.\r\n\r\n<img width=\"1150\" alt=\"Bildschirmfoto 2023-05-07 um 18 58 58\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/102828/236691962-e1b9b4a2-ed89-4fb4-8cb6-1854d4b04abb.png\">\r\n<img width=\"1140\" alt=\"Bildschirmfoto 2023-05-07 um 19 04 09\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/102828/236691969-bfeb84dc-6dce-4ab3-9048-02c6979ef2a0.png\">\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nTwo comments I already have: \r\n1. Is there a simple way to construct a _light_ theme from the colours I am already generating. Maybe an easy `reverse()` would work. Pretty sure that if I would start reading about colour theory again, I would learn that it's not just that easy.\r\n2. I wonder how I could make use of this module also outside neovim. [Wezterm](https://wezfurlong.org/wezterm/index.html) can execute Lua, or there could be a function that prints out the current generated colour scheme as configuration for a given tool. I like to have the same look in multiple tools I use throughout the day. Any thoughts about this one?",
