        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> 1. Is there a simple way to construct a _light_ theme from the colours I am already generating. Maybe an easy `reverse()` would work. Pretty sure that if I would start reading about colour theory again, I would learn that it's not just that easy.\r\n\r\nUse light background and dark foreground (usually with `saturation = 'high'`). If you want particular hue, I'd recommend using [Oklch interactive picker](https://bottosson.github.io/misc/colorpicker).\r\n\r\nFor Nord palette it seems to be `{ background = '#ECEFF4', foreground = '#434C5E' }`.\r\n\r\n> 2. I wonder how I could make use of this module also outside neovim. [Wezterm](https://wezfurlong.org/wezterm/index.html) can execute Lua, or there could be a function that prints out the current generated colour scheme as configuration for a given tool. I like to have the same look in multiple tools I use throughout the day. Any thoughts about this one?\r\n\r\nYes, there is `make_palette()`. Give it the config as input, and it will return all colors used (and slightly more). Try this, for example: `:= require('mini.hues').make_palette({ background = '#11262d', foreground = '#c0c8cc' })`. If you want it as text in regular buffer, there is `put_text` in 'mini.misc'.",
